    {
      "id": "piano.s8.l1",
      "title": "Natural minor and relative keys",
      "summary": "The minor scale's step pattern, and the major key that shares its notes.",
      "minutes": 12,
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "The natural minor scale",
          "body": "The **natural minor scale** uses the step pattern **W H W W H W W**. From A it uses only white keys: **A B C D E F G A**.\n\nCompared with a major scale on the same tonic, natural minor has a lowered 3rd, 6th, and 7th (in scale-degree terms: 1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7). The lowered 3rd is what makes it sound minor.",
          "diagram": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "scale": "A natural minor",
            "notes": [
              "A4",
              "B4",
              "C5",
              "D5",
              "E5",
              "F5",
              "G5",
              "A5"
            ],
            "labels": "degrees",
            "pitchRange": [
              "A4",
              "A5"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "Relative major and minor",
          "body": "A minor and C major use exactly the same notes and the same key signature (no sharps or flats). They are **relative** keys. Every major key has a **relative minor** that starts on its **6th degree** (three half steps below the tonic):\n\n| Major | Relative minor | Signature |\n|---|---|---|\n| C | A minor | none |\n| G | E minor | F♯ |\n| D | B minor | F♯ C♯ |\n| F | D minor | B♭ |\n| B♭ | G minor | B♭ E♭ |\n\nA **parallel** minor, by contrast, shares the tonic but not the signature: C major and C minor (three flats)."
        },
