    {
      "id": "piano.s8.l2",
      "title": "Harmonic and melodic minor",
      "summary": "Raise the 7th (and sometimes the 6th) for a stronger pull home.",
      "minutes": 13,
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "Harmonic minor",
          "body": "In natural minor, the 7th degree (G in A minor) is a whole step below the tonic, so it pulls home weakly. **Harmonic minor** raises the 7th by a half step: **A B C D E F G♯ A**.\n\nThis makes the V chord major (**E = E G♯ B**, and **E7 = E G♯ B D**), giving minor keys a strong V→i cadence. The gap from F to G♯ is 3 half steps, an **augmented 2nd**, which gives harmonic minor its distinctive sound.\n\nThe raised 7th is written as an accidental in the music; it is not in the key signature.",
          "diagram": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "scale": "A harmonic minor",
            "notes": [
              "A4",
              "B4",
              "C5",
              "D5",
              "E5",
              "F5",
              "G#5",
              "A5"
            ],
            "pitchRange": [
              "A4",
              "A5"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "Melodic minor",
          "body": "**Melodic minor** (in classical use) raises both the 6th and 7th going **up**, to avoid the augmented 2nd: **A B C D E F♯ G♯ A**. Going **down** it usually returns to natural minor: **A G F E D C B A**.\n\nFingering for A natural, harmonic, and melodic minor is the same as C major in both hands.",
