    {
      "id": "piano.s8.l4",
      "title": "The 12-bar blues",
      "summary": "The blues form, a boogie left hand, and the blues scale.",
      "minutes": 14,
      "steps": [
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "The form",
          "body": "The **12-bar blues** is a 12-measure chord pattern built on I, IV, and V, usually all played as **dominant seventh** chords. In C: **C7, F7, G7**.\n\n| Bars 1–4 | C7 | C7 | C7 | C7 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bars 5–8** | **F7** | **F7** | **C7** | **C7** |\n| **Bars 9–12** | **G7** | **F7** | **C7** | **G7** |\n\nThe G7 in bar 12 is a **turnaround**: it leads back to bar 1. On the last time through, end on C7.\n\nUsing a dominant seventh on I and IV breaks the major-key pattern from Stage 6 on purpose. That clash is part of the blues sound."
        },
        {
          "type": "explain",
          "title": "A boogie left hand",
          "body": "A classic LH pattern climbs the chord and comes back, in eighth notes: **1 3 5 6 ♭7 6 5 3**.\n\n- C7: C3 E3 G3 A3 B♭3 A3 G3 E3\n- F7: F2 A2 C3 D3 E♭3 D3 C3 A2\n- G7: G2 B2 D3 E3 F3 E3 D3 B2\n\nBlues is often played with **swing** (long–short eighths). Here the eighths are **straight** (even) so the timing check is exact.",
          "diagram": {
            "kind": "keyboard",
            "notes": [
              "C3",
              "E3",
              "G3",
              "A3",
              "Bb3"
            ],
            "pitchRange": [
              "C3",
              "C4"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "practice",
          "exercise": {
            "kind": "playSequence",
            "prompt": "Left hand boogie on C7, two measures.",
            "notes": [
              "C3",
              "E3",
